 \paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
 
-\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless. (ii) A matched comparison of persistent age-ranked targeting with verified-history targeting, and a factorial attribution of R3's three parts. (iii) Measured attack strength and failure boundaries: stronger and independently authored attackers, welfare attacks and coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair. (iv) Two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) Persistent age-ranked targeting is not materially better than verified-history targeting at the same budget: the development-selected history comparator is pooled-equivalent to R3 within the registered margin. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain. (iii) Episode-level mixtures of existing public-family attacks expose a welfare weakness that the registered profit-constrained search does not cover. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
