attack as companion, give estimated normalised gains of \site{r_ph_g_r3} and \site{r_ph_g_cs} and gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} and \site{r_ph_e_cs}; with the public D2+H3 attack as companion the efficiencies are \site{r_ph_e_r3_d} and \site{r_ph_e_cs_d} (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). The registered W1 constraint is a non-negative mean gain on the training seeds. Reconstructed from the search archives, the training mean gains of these mixtures, each at its rule--companion pair's weight in Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}, are \site{r_ph_t_r3} and \site{r_ph_t_r3_d} for R3 and \site{r_ph_t_cs} and \site{r_ph_t_cs_d} for C$^*$ (H4 and D2+H3 companions): three meet the training constraint, but C$^*$'s public-family mixture does not. At its evaluation-selected weight of \site{r_ph_wd_cs}, C$^*$'s public-family mixture fails the training constraint. At \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, the largest weight on the same grid at which both the training mean gain and the evaluation bound are non-negative, it has training gain \site{r_ph_ft_cs}, estimated evaluation gain \site{r_ph_fg_cs}, and gross efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs}, against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (last row of Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}). Both rules therefore show substantial empirical efficiency losses with public-family mixtures alone; H4 is not needed for a drop of this size, and population profitability remains unestablished. The weights are the largest at which a nominal one-sided \const{95} percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative; that bound is not adjusted for selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
