 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the registered margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks reduce the gross efficiency of every core rule, and against R3 and C$^*$ they earn less than the truthful reference; exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach substantially lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so the favourable profit-constrained welfare result does not extend to randomised policies, although their population profitability is not established. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
+For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the registered margin under both Q1 families. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search yields efficiency near \site{c_w1} for R3 and C$^*$; exploratory episode-level mixtures selected on the evaluation records yield \site{c_mx_r3} and \site{c_mx_cs} at non-negative estimated gain. The registered W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies; the population profitability of the selected mixtures remains unestablished. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
 % END-EV2
 
-\paragraph{Availability.} The release, at the report commit and signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2}, will contain the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, the deviation evidence and the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs with the per-seed inputs of the lottery analysis, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, and \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
+\paragraph{Availability.} The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, and \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
